course: ai
lesson: 9
title: The essay the teacher spotted
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Connor hands in an essay about "the breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean". He spent his holiday in rainy Skegness. His teacher knew straight away, because she knows how Connor writes and where he went.
explain: Homework is practice, like football training: if a bot does your keepy-uppies, you don't get better at keepy-uppies. Sam got full marks on bot homework and then failed the test.
explain: Used well, AI can be a patient tutor: explaining fractions a new way, quizzing your 7 and 8 times tables, or showing which spellings you got wrong so you can learn them.
explain: Used badly, it hands you invented "facts", like a volcano erupting in London, a prize that feels sick to hold, and a "brave" story that never happened to you.
explain: Schools are making rules about AI, like rules about calculators: read them, and when you are not sure, ask.
fact: Teachers know how their pupils usually write, so a sudden fancy style stands out.
fact: Chatbots have a typical style, with words like "furthermore" and "in conclusion", that teachers learn to notice.
fact: Using AI to understand an idea, then doing the work yourself, is like having a patient tutor.
fact: Homework is practice; copying it skips the learning and shows up in tests.
fact: Many schools now have rules for AI; written work usually must be the pupil's own.
fact: Using a bot to find spelling mistakes is useful if you learn from the corrections.
fact: Bots can hallucinate facts, so every fact in a project must be checked; no volcano has erupted in London.
fact: A prize won with someone else's work does not feel like winning; owning up is the brave thing.
fact: Personal writing must come from your own life, because only you know your own stories.
fact: A bot can be a good practice partner, for example quizzing times tables.
fact: Copying rules get stricter as you get older, and universities can punish AI copying.
exercise: Connor's essay mentions "breathtaking vistas of the Mediterranean". He went to Skegness. What gave him away? | The words and the place didn't match his real life or style | Skegness is on the Mediterranean, so that part was right, but it was too long | Teachers can read the bot's mind | The essay was too short
exercise: Mrs Patel spots "furthermore" and "in conclusion" from a pupil who writes "and then". Why does that stand out? | Bots have a typical style, and it isn't his | Those long words are banned in all primary schools | Pupils are not allowed to know long words | Mrs Patel dislikes long words
exercise: Lily uses the bot to explain fractions differently, then does the questions herself. Is that a good use? | Yes, it's using AI to learn, not to copy | No, any use of AI at all is cheating, even to learn | No, she should copy the answers too | Yes, but only if the bot does half
exercise: Sam got full marks on bot-done homework, then failed the test. Why? | Homework is practice, and he skipped the practice | The test was much harder than the rules allow for his age | The teacher marked him unfairly | Bots are banned from tests, so he forgot
exercise: Chloe's school letter says AI may be used for research with permission, but written work must be the pupil's own. What should she do? | Follow the rules, and ask if unsure | Ignore the letter, because bots are fine for everything | Use the bot for everything secretly | Never open a computer again
exercise: The bot fixes three of Maddie's spellings, and she writes them in her notebook. Why is that smart? | She learns from the corrections | She can show everyone the bot was right | She gets extra marks for keeping a notebook | She saves the bot from forgetting them
exercise: Jake's volcano project says a volcano erupted in London in 1850. What went wrong? | The bot made it up and Jake didn't check | A volcano really erupted in London that year | The date should have been 1950 | London had a volcano that is now hidden
exercise: Mia wins the poetry prize with a bot's poem and feels sick. What should she do? | Tell the teacher, and next time write her own | Keep quiet and enter again next year with another one | Blame the bot for entering it | Give the prize to the bot
exercise: Connor's "time I was brave" essay from the bot is about climbing a mountain. What is wrong? | It isn't his story; only he knows his real brave moments | Mountains are not brave enough for an essay about bravery | The essay should be about a lion | Nothing, made-up stories count as his
exercise: Lily asks the bot to quiz her on her 7s and 8s. Good use? | Yes, a bot can be a good practice partner | No, quizzes must be done on paper only | No, bots don't know times tables | Yes, if it also does all her homework
exercise: Sam's sister used AI for her university essay and must retake the module. What does this show? | Copying gets more serious as you get older | Universities don't mind AI copying at all | Only children get punished for copying | Retaking is a reward for hard work
exercise: What is homework mainly for? | Practising so you get better | Keeping teachers busy every evening | Proving that bots are cleverer than you | Filling up your evening so you're not bored
exercise: How are school AI rules like calculator rules? | Allowed for some things, not others; follow them | Both are banned everywhere, in every school, forever | Both are only for teachers | Neither has any rules at all
exercise: What should you do with every fact in a project? | Check it in a book or trusted website | Copy it in quickly before you forget it | Make it longer so it sounds more clever | Leave it out only if it is interesting
test: "teachers can't tell, the bot writes way better than me" Why might they tell? | They know how you write, and the bot's style is different | Teachers wear special AI glasses that show them who really wrote it | They can't, it's impossible | They phone the bot company every time
test: What would happen if Sam kept using the bot for all his maths homework? | He wouldn't practise, and tests would go badly | He would get better at maths anyway, just by reading | The bot would sit his tests too | His teacher would give him extra marks
test: "my project says a volcano blew up in London in 1850, the bot said" What should Jake do? | Check it; there was no such eruption | Keep it, because bots know their history | Change the date to 1851 | Add a picture to make it believable
test: A cartoon kid gets a gold medal for a bot poem and grins forever. What does the cartoon miss? | A prize you didn't earn feels empty | Bot poems always win medals in the end | Teachers never notice where poems come from | Medals are only for bots, not for people
test: Lily doesn't get fractions from the textbook. What is a good plan? | Ask the bot to explain differently, then try the questions herself | Ask the bot to do the questions and then copy them out neatly | Skip fractions forever | Copy her friend's answers
test: "my essay about the time i was brave? just let the bot write one" What is the problem? | Only you know your real stories | Bots aren't allowed to write about bravery | The bot will write a story about you exactly | It's fine, made-up is better
test: "uni don't care about AI, it's only school that's strict" Is that right? | No, universities have strict rules too | Yes, at uni anything goes, you can use what you like | Yes, only primary schools mind | No, but only for maths
test: Maddie's bot fixes her spelling. What turns that into learning? | Looking at what was fixed and why | Clicking "accept all" quickly and moving on | Never looking at the changes it made | Asking the bot to fix it all twice over
test: Your school letter doesn't mention AI drawing tools. What should you do before using one? | Ask the teacher first | Use it, anything not banned is fine | Use it but hide it | Use it only at night
test: Which is the best use of AI for times tables? | Being quizzed and then checking tricky ones | Asking for all the answers so you can copy them | Never learning them because bots know | Asking the bot to sit the test
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of homework? | The provision of practice whereby the pupil's skills are developed | The occupation of the pupil's evening without further object | The demonstration of the capabilities of chatbots | The supply of material for the teacher's amusement
exam: It is given that a pupil who customarily writes in a plain manner submits an essay replete with expressions such as "furthermore". Identify the principal reason the teacher may suspect assistance. | The style departs markedly from the pupil's established manner of writing | Such expressions are prohibited in schools | Teachers possess instruments that read the chatbot's memory directly | The essay exceeds the permitted length
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a project asserting a volcanic eruption in London in 1850. | The assertion is false and indicates an unverified fabrication | The assertion is true and well documented in the history books | The assertion is true save for the date | The assertion cannot in principle be verified
exam: Ascertain the use of a chatbot which accords with honest learning. | Obtaining an alternative explanation, followed by independent completion of the exercises | Obtaining completed answers for submission as one's own | Obtaining a poem for entry in a competition under one's own name | Obtaining a personal narrative of events which never occurred
